# Secrets (local environment files are never committed)
.env.local

# Deployment targets carry a 12-digit AWS account id, which
# `tests/hygiene/test_repo_hygiene.py` refuses in the working tree *and in all
# git history*. The AgentCore CLI's own scaffold commits this file, which is
# reasonable for a private team repository; this one is public. Copy
# `aws-targets.json.example`, fill in the account, and keep it local — the
# same pattern as `.env.example` at the repository root.
aws-targets.json

# CDK Build Artifacts
cdk/cdk.out/
cdk/node_modules/

# npm's lock file carries third-party package metadata, and `glob`'s
# deprecation notice embeds its maintainer's email address — which
# `tests/hygiene/test_repo_hygiene.py` refuses, correctly, since it cannot tell
# a stranger's address from one of ours. That check's allow-list is built
# around a single known placeholder and extending it for a generated file we
# do not author would widen a control that has already earned its keep.
#
# The reproducibility cost is small: the CLI rewrites `cdk/package.json` on
# every deploy (it reported alpha.45 → alpha.53 on the first dry run), the
# AgentCore dependency is an exact pin, and the rest are patch ranges.
cdk/package-lock.json

# CLI Internals
.cli/*

# Ephemeral Staging
.cache/*

# The CLI's own scaffold makes an exception here and commits the deployed
# state, which is right for a private team repository: it records which
# runtime each target points at. Ours carries the runtime and role ARNs, and
# an ARN embeds the 12-digit account id that
# `tests/hygiene/test_repo_hygiene.py` refuses in the working tree and in all
# git history. Same trade as `aws-targets.json` above.
#
# What the file records is not lost: `docs/evidence/` carries the deployment
# with the account masked, and `agentcore status` reads the live truth from
# AWS rather than from this cache.
#
# (No `!.cli/deployed-state.json` exception, deliberately — `.cli/*` above
# already covers it.)
